  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "For $\\triangle ABC$ we are given that $c=6$ and $\\angle A=25^\\circ$. Find the value(s) of $a$ for which there is only one such triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=6\\sin25^\\circ$ or $a\\ge6$.\n\nThe height boundary gives one right-angled triangle; equal or longer opposite sides give one triangle.",
      "worked": "The limiting height is\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=c\\sin A\\\\h&=6\\sin25^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $a<h$, no triangle exists. If $a=h$, exactly one right-angled triangle exists. If $h<a<6$, two triangles exist.\n\nWhen $a=6$, the sine rule gives $C=25^\\circ$ or $155^\\circ$. The latter gives $B=0^\\circ$, so only $C=25^\\circ$, $B=130^\\circ$ is valid.\n\nWhen $a>6$,\n$$\\sin C=\\frac{6\\sin25^\\circ}{a}<\\sin25^\\circ.$$\nThe acute solution has $C<25^\\circ$; its supplementary solution has $C>155^\\circ$ and cannot form a triangle with $A=25^\\circ$. Thus exactly one triangle exists.\n\nTherefore $a=6\\sin25^\\circ$ or $a\\ge6$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
